WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.760
Starship Flight 15 Preview The first ship catch attempt aims to catch an orbital ship at the tower for the first time. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 158. This special is the briefing before launch day. After the plan on the pad, the minute by minute, why the ship is the hard catch, and a scale for scoring whatever happens. SpaceX plans Starship Flight 15 as the first flight that tries to catch both stages at the tower. The super heavy booster on the way back

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.120
and then, more than 22 hours later, the ship after it has reached orbit.

00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:41.365
The attempt is targeted no earlier than Friday, November 13, 2026, from Starbase Pad 2,

00:00:41.885 --> 00:00:44.645
and every date in this episode is a target that can move.

00:00:45.185 --> 00:00:48.905
You would see a change on SpaceX's official Flight 15 mission page.

00:00:49.365 --> 00:00:52.285
This preview is recorded before the vehicle leaves the pad.

00:00:52.845 --> 00:00:54.825
Nothing about Flight 15 is a result.

00:00:55.405 --> 00:00:59.905
Your job on the day is to score what you see against a clear plan, not against a

00:00:59.905 --> 00:01:06.505
press release. As of November 10th, that page lists a 75-minute window opening at 4.45 pm

00:01:06.505 --> 00:01:14.725
central time, 5.45 pm eastern, and closing at 6 pm central, roughly 22.45 to midnight UTC.

00:01:15.365 --> 00:01:21.805
No fixed T0 has been announced. The schedule is dynamic. A slip would be normal. SpaceX vice

00:01:21.805 --> 00:01:26.805
president of launch Kiko Donchev had already pointed at November 13th at the UP summit,

00:01:26.805 --> 00:01:32.865
and the live page still matches. The launch is pending regulatory approval. That is the wording

00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:38.745
on the SpaceX mission page as of checks between November 7th and 11th. A November 10th secondary

00:01:38.745 --> 00:01:44.925
article claimed the license was already in hand. That claim is unconfirmed and it conflicts with SpaceX's

00:01:44.925 --> 00:01:52.905
own pending language. The vehicles are booster 22 and ship 42, both Block 3, also called V3 hardware.

00:01:52.905 --> 00:01:57.685
Booster 22 is the first V3 super heavy plan for a tower catch.

00:01:58.145 --> 00:02:01.165
Ship 42 is the V3 ship assigned to this flight.

00:02:01.705 --> 00:02:04.385
The payload is 26 star-linked V3 satellites.

00:02:04.965 --> 00:02:07.505
Three of them carry cameras to inspect the heat shield.

00:02:08.085 --> 00:02:11.805
The plan profile in order is lift off from star-based pad 2.

00:02:12.405 --> 00:02:16.165
Hot staging, which is the ship lighting while the two stages are still attached.

00:02:16.665 --> 00:02:20.585
The super heavy booster returning for a catch at the pad 2 mechazilla tower.

00:02:20.585 --> 00:02:25.005
Those are the chopsticks, the arms that squeeze and grab structural hard points.

00:02:25.465 --> 00:02:30.005
Then the ship continuing toward low Earth orbit and attempting a return catch at the

00:02:30.005 --> 00:02:35.245
same tower only after systems checks confirm it is ready for reentry and the landing burn.

00:02:36.005 --> 00:02:38.525
Ocean off ramps are already written into that plan.

00:02:39.547 --> 00:02:44.627
Treat the times that follow as the plan timeline SpaceX has published, not as a promise.

00:02:45.227 --> 00:02:48.627
They come from the Flight 15 mission page and its timeline table.

00:02:48.627 --> 00:02:55.867
lift off is the super heavy booster lighting its engines under ship 42 stacked on pad 2 the stack

00:02:55.867 --> 00:03:02.027
climbs hot staging is planned around T plus two minutes and 21 seconds the ship lights while it

00:03:02.027 --> 00:03:07.187
is still attached the stage is separate and the super heavy booster begins the work of coming home

00:03:07.727 --> 00:03:13.467
that super heavy booster never intended to reach orbit its job on this flight is a high energy hop

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:18.487
up, then a boost back, it turns around and burns toward the coast, then a landing burn.

00:03:19.027 --> 00:03:22.927
The landing burn is planned to start around T plus 6 minutes and 10 seconds.

00:03:23.587 --> 00:03:26.987
The catch is planned around T plus 6 minutes and 35 seconds.

00:03:27.547 --> 00:03:30.447
This would be the first catch attempt for a V3 super heavy.

00:03:31.047 --> 00:03:33.507
After staging, ship 42 is on its own.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:35.767
The plan is not a blind insertion.

00:03:36.247 --> 00:03:40.247
The mission page describes a passively safe suborbital trajectory at first.

00:03:40.247 --> 00:03:43.407
A path that falls back to Earth if nothing else is done.

00:03:43.947 --> 00:03:46.387
With orbit insertion only if the vehicle is cleared.

00:03:46.907 --> 00:03:52.647
If it is cleared, the ship targets about 275 kilometers, deploys the payload, and stays

00:03:52.647 --> 00:03:54.167
up for about 14 orbits.

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:59.427
Coverage is planned through the ship catch, with possible extended starlink views from orbit.

00:04:00.007 --> 00:04:02.987
While it is up there, the vehicle is not just a payload truck.

00:04:03.547 --> 00:04:07.707
The cameras on those satellites are there so the company can look at the heat shield and

00:04:07.707 --> 00:04:10.087
and decide whether the ship is healthy enough to bring home.

00:04:10.687 --> 00:04:12.747
That decision point is the hinge of the flight.

00:04:13.327 --> 00:04:18.087
SpaceX has said the ship catch will be attempted only if ship systems for reentry and landing

00:04:18.087 --> 00:04:21.087
are confirmed healthy after those on-orbit checks.

00:04:21.707 --> 00:04:27.347
If they are not, vehicle health, tower, weather, or any other reason, the catch is called off

00:04:27.347 --> 00:04:29.987
and the ship is aimed at a contingency ocean landing.

00:04:30.607 --> 00:04:34.847
Treat a called off catch as a planned off-ramp, not as an improvised failure.

00:04:34.847 --> 00:04:40.187
here. The company built those off ramps in. The super heavy boosters water option is the

00:04:40.187 --> 00:04:45.427
Gulf of Mexico. The ship's options are the Indian Ocean and the Pacific, with multiple

00:04:45.427 --> 00:04:50.847
predetermined safe deorbit areas. If the ship is cleared, the deorbit burn comes late.

00:04:51.487 --> 00:04:56.867
The burn that drops it out of orbit. Reentry is on the timeline around 22 hours, 10 minutes,

00:04:57.147 --> 00:05:02.787
and 46 seconds. The landing burn, the flip, and the catch are on the timeline around 22

00:05:02.787 --> 00:05:06.207
hours, 32 minutes, and 34 seconds, if cleared.

00:05:06.807 --> 00:05:12.207
The ship has to survive heating, keep control, relight, flip from a belly-first re-entry

00:05:12.207 --> 00:05:17.667
attitude into a landing attitude, and arrive at the same pad, two arms that, if all has

00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:21.367
gone well, already caught the super-heavy booster the evening before.

00:05:21.967 --> 00:05:24.627
Every recovery in that sequence names a stage.

00:05:25.107 --> 00:05:28.587
The super-heavy booster on the short loop, the ship on the long one.

00:05:28.587 --> 00:05:34.446
From an engineering standpoint, those two tower catches use the same arms and are not the

00:05:34.446 --> 00:05:35.126
same problem.

00:05:35.626 --> 00:05:39.626
The difference is energy, and energy is why the ship's return is a different kind

00:05:39.626 --> 00:05:40.246
of engineering.

00:05:40.846 --> 00:05:43.066
The super heavy booster does not go to orbit.

00:05:43.606 --> 00:05:48.346
It lifts the stack, stages, turns around, and comes back from a suborbital trajectory.

00:05:49.066 --> 00:05:51.346
It is fast, and catching it is not easy.

00:05:51.906 --> 00:05:56.546
SpaceX has already shown that a booster catch can work, and it has also shown that it can

00:05:56.546 --> 00:05:56.846
fail.

00:05:56.846 --> 00:06:00.206
oil, but the booster never has to dump the energy of being an orbit.

00:06:00.686 --> 00:06:01.366
The ship does.

00:06:02.026 --> 00:06:03.806
Orbital velocity is a different regime.

00:06:04.426 --> 00:06:08.526
To stay an orbit, the ship has to be moving so fast that missing the earth is the default.

00:06:09.146 --> 00:06:11.386
To come home, that speed has to go somewhere.

00:06:11.966 --> 00:06:13.846
In the atmosphere, somewhere is heat.

00:06:14.346 --> 00:06:17.986
The heat shield's job is to take that energy so the vehicle structure does not.

00:06:18.606 --> 00:06:23.366
Tiles on the windward side, the face that hits the airflow, are the barrier between plasma

00:06:23.366 --> 00:06:24.066
and steel.

00:06:24.066 --> 00:06:29.506
fuel. Flight 14 already flew with reused heat shield tiles from an earlier vehicle, which

00:06:29.506 --> 00:06:34.226
is one reason this attempt is even on the table. The company has at least some flight evidence

00:06:34.226 --> 00:06:39.946
that the shield can be flown, recovered in some form, and flown again. SpaceX wants to

00:06:39.946 --> 00:06:44.706
know how the tiles look before it commits the ship to reentry and a catch. That is what

00:06:44.706 --> 00:06:50.486
the on-orbit inspection is for. Control through reentry is the next problem. A booster returning

00:06:50.486 --> 00:06:55.246
from a hop is in thicker air sooner, with a different heating pulse and a different control

00:06:55.246 --> 00:07:00.206
task. A ship coming from orbit spends a long time in a regime where the air is thin.

00:07:00.826 --> 00:07:04.886
The heating is intense, and the plasma around the vehicle can interfere with communications

00:07:04.886 --> 00:07:10.086
and with the clean picture a guidance computer wants. The vehicle has to hold an attitude

00:07:10.086 --> 00:07:15.066
that keeps the heat shield into the flow. Then, low and late, it has to change its mind.

00:07:15.066 --> 00:07:20.466
Re-light engines, flip from that belly-first attitude into a tail-down landing attitude,

00:07:20.946 --> 00:07:25.146
and put itself in front of the tower arms with almost no extra propellant to waste on

00:07:25.146 --> 00:07:25.826
a go-around.

00:07:26.506 --> 00:07:30.806
Landing legs would have given a last resort pad or ocean landing with more forgiveness.

00:07:31.566 --> 00:07:34.606
This architecture does not carry that forgiveness on the vehicle.

00:07:35.266 --> 00:07:36.606
The arms are the landing gear.

00:07:37.206 --> 00:07:40.066
That is also why SpaceX wants tower catches at all.

00:07:40.546 --> 00:07:44.966
Legs are dry mass, structure you fly that is not payload and not propellant.

00:07:45.646 --> 00:07:50.346
Dry mass on an upper stage is payload you did not carry, or propellant you cannot use

00:07:50.346 --> 00:07:51.086
to go farther.

00:07:51.726 --> 00:07:55.726
Catching on the tower means the ship and the super heavy booster can be designed without

00:07:55.726 --> 00:07:59.706
landing legs, which cuts the mass that has to be flown on every mission.

00:08:00.306 --> 00:08:04.726
It also means the vehicle comes back to the pad at left, in the hardware that can stack

00:08:04.726 --> 00:08:09.866
it again, which is the path to a fast restack rather than a recovery fleet and a road trip.

00:08:09.866 --> 00:08:15.386
ship. The arms catch by squeezing structural hardpoints. A method SpaceX has tested with

00:08:15.386 --> 00:08:20.626
suspended vehicles and with prior super heavy booster catches. Arriving at those hardpoints

00:08:20.626 --> 00:08:25.946
after an orbital reentry, a flip, and a landing burn is the part that has no flight heritage

00:08:25.946 --> 00:08:31.226
on a ship. The super heavy booster catch on this flight deserves attention. It is not the

00:08:31.226 --> 00:08:36.626
hard part in kind. The ship catches. The ship is the stage that went all the way to orbital

00:08:36.626 --> 00:08:40.466
velocity, and orbital velocity is what the atmosphere has to erase.

00:08:41.544 --> 00:08:43.624
The program did not jump to a dual catch.

00:08:44.284 --> 00:08:49.304
The super heavy booster catch was proven first on earlier V1 flights three times.

00:08:49.964 --> 00:08:53.024
Those catches are the reason pad 2's arms are not a theory.

00:08:53.584 --> 00:08:59.484
They are also not a copy-paste onto V3, which is why booster 22's catch, if it is attempted,

00:08:59.844 --> 00:09:01.384
is still a first for this block.

00:09:02.044 --> 00:09:05.504
Flight 14 is the immediate predecessor this preview has to carry.

00:09:05.504 --> 00:09:10.604
As this show has reported, that flight reached orbit and deployed the same satellite count

00:09:10.604 --> 00:09:15.884
Flight 15 is assigned, despite an engine shutdown that Elon Musk described as a software

00:09:15.884 --> 00:09:19.664
failure on a single R-VAC unit, a Raptor vacuum engine.

00:09:20.264 --> 00:09:22.864
What Flight 14 did not do is catch the ship.

00:09:23.384 --> 00:09:24.384
That is the open item.

00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:27.944
The test campaign for these two vehicles is the last mile.

00:09:28.504 --> 00:09:35.084
Booster 22's full duration 33 engine static fire on October 25, engines lit at full set

00:09:35.084 --> 00:09:39.564
while the vehicle is held down, is the ground evidence that the booster can run the set.

00:09:40.144 --> 00:09:46.064
Ship 42's six engine static fire and single engine deorbit simulation on October 28th

00:09:46.064 --> 00:09:49.744
are the ground evidence that the ship can light the engines it will need to come home.

00:09:50.364 --> 00:09:56.324
Final catch testing on the pad, including the squeeze test with booster 22 as of about November 10th,

00:09:56.464 --> 00:10:01.804
is the tower side of the same argument. The arms have to be ready to grab a V3 super heavy

00:10:01.804 --> 00:10:04.644
and later a ship by those hard points.

00:10:05.164 --> 00:10:10.004
Earlier reporting that the pad two arms had already lifted ship 42 into catch position,

00:10:10.484 --> 00:10:11.704
sits in the same campaign.

00:10:12.324 --> 00:10:13.224
None of that is flight.

00:10:13.724 --> 00:10:18.044
It is why the company is willing to put a catch of both stages on the flight 15 plan.

00:10:18.604 --> 00:10:24.124
No verified slips, scrubs, or major new anomalies were announced between November 7th and 10th.

00:10:24.964 --> 00:10:26.124
The spectacle is the catch.

00:10:26.724 --> 00:10:28.544
What rides on it is the reuse loop.

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:35.844
space X frames full and rapid reuse of both stages as the path to cutting cost to orbit by 99% or more.

00:10:36.444 --> 00:10:41.024
That is a company projection, not a measured result, and it should be heard as a projection.

00:10:41.644 --> 00:10:46.884
The logic underneath it is the engineering of the arms. If neither stage is thrown away and if neither

00:10:46.884 --> 00:10:51.724
stage carries landing legs, and if both come back to the tower that can stack them again,

00:10:52.224 --> 00:10:57.624
the cost of a flight starts to look like propellant, operations, and refurbishment rather than building a

00:10:57.624 --> 00:11:00.104
new super heavy booster and a new ship every time.

00:11:00.804 --> 00:11:02.684
Flight 15 is not that factory.

00:11:03.264 --> 00:11:07.684
It is the attempt to show that the recovery half of that sentence can be done on one flight,

00:11:08.124 --> 00:11:11.184
for both stages, with the ship coming home from orbit.

00:11:11.744 --> 00:11:16.564
Starlink is the payload that makes the attempt commercially live, not a mass simulator.

00:11:17.224 --> 00:11:22.464
Earlier reporting from KeepTrack put each V3 satellite at an added terabyte of capacity,

00:11:22.464 --> 00:11:26.944
and this set at roughly 10 Falcon 9 launches of V2 mini satellites.

00:11:27.584 --> 00:11:29.704
A terabit is a trillion bits a second.

00:11:30.224 --> 00:11:33.304
Think of one of these satellites as a fat fiber line in the sky,

00:11:33.844 --> 00:11:38.644
and this load as about 10 older Falcon 9 starlink missions stacked into a single ship.

00:11:39.224 --> 00:11:43.464
A ship that can deploy that and then be caught is a different production story from a ship

00:11:43.464 --> 00:11:45.764
that can deploy that and then ditch in the ocean.

00:11:46.424 --> 00:11:51.044
Even a successful deploy with a called off catch still puts V3 hardware on orbit.

00:11:51.044 --> 00:11:56.004
The catch is what turns the upper stage from a costly expendable into a vehicle that

00:11:56.004 --> 00:12:01.304
can fly again. Artemis and the company's own moon and Mars plans sit behind that same

00:12:01.304 --> 00:12:06.124
reuse loop. This briefing does not have a new NASA milestone tied to this attempt,

00:12:06.624 --> 00:12:12.604
and it should not invent one. The honest version is the one SpaceX already uses. A rapidly reusable

00:12:12.604 --> 00:12:17.004
two-stage system is the architecture they say they need for the cadence those programs

00:12:17.004 --> 00:12:22.204
times imply. A first-ship catch would be a single data point on that architecture, not

00:12:22.204 --> 00:12:28.004
the architecture itself. The business thread is secondary and stays that way. SPCX is

00:12:28.004 --> 00:12:33.064
the listed company. This research does not report a flight-15 specific market move that

00:12:33.064 --> 00:12:38.632
belongs in this briefing. The engineering is the headline. One thing worth watching is that

00:12:38.632 --> 00:12:43.712
this is a test flight with a catch on the plan, not a routine operational launch with a catch

00:12:43.712 --> 00:12:50.092
as a footnote. Score it that way. The off-ramp SpaceX has named are the first ledger. A super

00:12:50.092 --> 00:12:54.812
heavy booster catch can be called off for vehicle health, tower, weather, or other reasons.

00:12:55.372 --> 00:13:00.612
A ship catch is attempted only if reentry and landing systems are confirmed healthy after on

00:13:00.612 --> 00:13:07.092
orbit checks. Orbit insertion itself is gated on that passively safe path first. A called off

00:13:07.092 --> 00:13:12.912
catch on either stage is a decision the company has already written down. It can still be a disappointment.

00:13:12.912 --> 00:13:14.752
It is not an unplanned mode.

00:13:15.172 --> 00:13:19.712
The risks that follow from the physics are the ones this preview can name without inventing

00:13:19.712 --> 00:13:20.492
failure reports.

00:13:21.092 --> 00:13:26.032
The super heavy booster has to light, stage, boost back, and arrive at the arms as a

00:13:26.032 --> 00:13:27.812
V3 vehicle for the first time.

00:13:28.352 --> 00:13:33.952
The ship has to insert, deploy, survive the long coast, pass the health checks, re-enter

00:13:33.952 --> 00:13:38.632
on the heat shield, relight, flip, and arrive at the same arms with little margin.

00:13:38.632 --> 00:13:44.852
Heat shield damage, an engine that does not relight, a guidance error on the flip, a tower

00:13:44.852 --> 00:13:49.712
that is not ready, weather, and a hold before T0 are all in the envelope.

00:13:50.432 --> 00:13:53.592
Elon Musk's own numbers treat the ship catch as uncertain.

00:13:54.332 --> 00:13:57.312
Those are his predictions, not odds this show is adopting.

00:13:57.972 --> 00:14:03.492
A clear scale of outcomes from the top down, full success is both catches plus the deploy.

00:14:04.192 --> 00:14:06.372
Super heavy booster caught on the short loop.

00:14:06.372 --> 00:14:13.132
the satellites on orbit ship caught after re-entry. Next is a super heavy booster catch, a successful

00:14:13.132 --> 00:14:19.212
deploy, and a healthy ship that is waved off to the ocean. That is still a huge flight. It proves

00:14:19.212 --> 00:14:26.072
V3 booster recovery and V3 satellite delivery. It leaves the ship catch open. Next is a super heavy

00:14:26.072 --> 00:14:31.632
booster catch and a ship that does not make a clean orbital mission. Insertion not cleared or a

00:14:31.632 --> 00:14:36.312
problem on orbit. The booster side of the dual catch plan would still have been attempted.

00:14:36.932 --> 00:14:41.792
Next is an ocean landing or loss of the super heavy booster with a ship that still completes the

00:14:41.792 --> 00:14:47.092
orbital piece. The payload mission can succeed while the first V3 booster catch does not.

00:14:47.732 --> 00:14:53.552
An early loss on a scent at staging or before the super heavy booster is back teaches whatever

00:14:53.552 --> 00:14:58.852
the telemetry gives and delays everything that was stacked on this attempt. A test flight that

00:14:58.852 --> 00:15:00.932
fails informatively is still a test.

00:15:01.512 --> 00:15:02.972
That sentence is not an excuse.

00:15:03.492 --> 00:15:07.992
It is the difference between a program that flies to learn and a press release that needed

00:15:07.992 --> 00:15:08.832
a perfect day.

00:15:09.472 --> 00:15:13.612
Whatever happens, you should be able to point at this scale and say which rung you saw.

00:15:14.703 --> 00:15:18.863
Elon Musk's comments are the ones with the most weight because they are his and because

00:15:18.863 --> 00:15:20.723
he put numbers on a first ship catch.

00:15:21.343 --> 00:15:26.143
He previously estimated success probability at at least 50% or 60%.

00:15:26.143 --> 00:15:32.223
or more likely than not, if Flight 14 succeeded, with backups available. On the third quarter

00:15:32.223 --> 00:15:37.623
earnings call he used more likely than not for a successful ship catch. Recent X activity,

00:15:37.783 --> 00:15:43.123
including his reposts of mission videos, shows continued promotion of the flight. The research

00:15:43.123 --> 00:15:48.343
does not show an updated numerical estimate from him in this briefing. Treat every one of those

00:15:48.343 --> 00:15:54.103
figures as a prediction by Musk, not as a measurement. SpaceX's own framing on the mission materials

00:15:54.103 --> 00:15:58.643
is that Flight 15 is a potential first full recovery of an orbital launch system.

00:15:59.123 --> 00:16:02.963
That is a company claim about what the attempt would mean, not a result.

00:16:03.663 --> 00:16:07.143
Gwen Shotwell's earlier comment that she expected the license to cover

00:16:07.143 --> 00:16:10.343
return to launch site and a tower catch for both stages

00:16:10.343 --> 00:16:13.703
is the most important regulatory note in this briefing.

00:16:14.323 --> 00:16:16.983
And it sits next to the live page still saying pending.

00:16:17.403 --> 00:16:19.223
On the prediction market side,

00:16:19.223 --> 00:16:25.603
That takes such as polymarket odds on catch success around 47-51% for the ship, and

00:16:25.603 --> 00:16:27.943
higher for the super heavy booster are opinion.

00:16:28.583 --> 00:16:33.163
They show how uncertain even sympathetic observers are about the ship catch in particular.

00:16:33.783 --> 00:16:37.083
They are not engineering analysis, and they are not this show's view.

00:16:37.783 --> 00:16:41.483
The sharpest technical argument in the reporting is not a personality fight.

00:16:42.043 --> 00:16:45.843
It is the dual catch explainer logic from outlets such as the space road.

00:16:45.843 --> 00:16:51.663
The arms and the squeeze are a known method. The super heavy booster catch has V1 heritage.

00:16:52.223 --> 00:16:58.823
The ship catch is a first because orbital reentry, the heat shield, and the flip to hover are a different energy problem.

00:16:59.423 --> 00:17:00.923
That is the argument worth having.

00:17:01.683 --> 00:17:05.123
Noise about dates and licenses without a primary source is not.

00:17:06.215 --> 00:17:09.975
Watch on the official SpaceX Flight 15 mission page and on X.

00:17:10.595 --> 00:17:15.735
The webcast is planned to start about 30 minutes before liftoff and to continue through the ship catch.

00:17:15.735 --> 00:17:20.775
match. How to watch reporting from the space road matches that. Four moments are worth

00:17:20.775 --> 00:17:26.755
watching for in order. First, hot staging, the stack becoming two vehicles. Second, the

00:17:26.755 --> 00:17:31.095
super heavy boosters landing burn and catch, or a call to send that booster to the golf.

00:17:31.735 --> 00:17:37.755
